  ('da8f4561-e51c-4dba-9cc8-dd5ec79d0edf', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses ligase (ATP-driven joining) with lyase (ATP-independent bond formation/elimination)'),
  ('da8f4561-e51c-4dba-9cc8-dd5ec79d0edf', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats isomerase as a catch-all rearrangement class and ignores that two substrates are combined'),
  ('da8f4561-e51c-4dba-9cc8-dd5ec79d0edf', 'D', 'misconception', 'sees ATP consumed and assumes a phosphate group is being transferred, defaulting to transferase');

-- Q25 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] modes=['A', 'B5'] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher characterizes the first enzyme of a five-step biosynthetic pathway. The pathway''s final product accumulates and slows the enzyme. Kinetic assays show that adding excess substrate does not relieve this slowdown, and binding studies place the final product at a pocket well away from the enzyme''s active site. No covalent group is added to the enzyme, and its molecular mass is unchanged whether the product is present or absent. Which mechanism is most consistent with these observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Allosteric feedback inhibition by the end product"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Competitive inhibition at the enzyme''s active site"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reversible covalent modification of the enzyme"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Proteolytic activation of an inactive zymogen"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The textbook describes feedback inhibition as a classic allosteric mechanism in which ''the end product of the pathway... binds to an allosteric site'' on the first enzyme and inhibits it, and it stresses that a molecule acting at a site ''distinct from the active site'' produces allosteric (not active-site) regulation. Because the final product binds a pocket away from the active site, is not out-competed by excess substrate, and adds no covalent group (unchanged mass), the data point to allosteric feedback inhibition. (Choice A) is correct: an end product binding a distal regulatory site, unrelieved by added substrate and adding no covalent group, is the textbook definition of allosteric feedback inhibition of a pathway''s first enzyme. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that any small-molecule inhibitor competes at the active site, but a competitive inhibitor binds the active site and its effect is overcome by excess substrate, whereas here excess substrate does not relieve inhibition and the product binds away from the active site. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that regulation by a metabolite must involve a phosphoryl or other covalent group; covalent modification would change the enzyme''s mass and requires a modifying enzyme, but the data show unchanged mass and only reversible ligand binding. (Choice D) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that turning enzyme output down is like zymogen control, but zymogen activation is a one-way proteolytic cleavage that turns an inactive precursor on, which would lower molecular mass and cannot account for reversible slowing by a bound product. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the criteria that distinguish allosteric, competitive, covalent, and proteolytic regulation to the binding-site, substrate-competition, and molecular-mass evidence provided.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'dacdc7f1-09a2-4f69-b8c1-79072898cd79';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'dacdc7f1-09a2-4f69-b8c1-79072898cd79';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('dacdc7f1-09a2-4f69-b8c1-79072898cd79', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes any metabolite inhibitor binds the active site and competes with substrate'),
  ('dacdc7f1-09a2-4f69-b8c1-79072898cd79', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes small-molecule regulation requires a covalent modification like phosphorylation'),
  ('dacdc7f1-09a2-4f69-b8c1-79072898cd79', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates reversible metabolite control with one-way proteolytic zymogen activation');

-- Q26 [Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots] modes=['A'] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher assays a purified enzyme at varying [S] and constructs a Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) plot of \(1/v_0\) versus \(1/[S]\). She then repeats the assay after adding a fixed concentration of a reversible inhibitor and plots a second line on the same axes. The two lines intersect exactly on the \(1/v_0\) axis (they share the same y-intercept), but the inhibited line''s x-intercept has moved closer to the origin (from \(-0.5\ \text{mM}^{-1}\) to \(-0.2\ \text{mM}^{-1}\)). Which type of inhibition best accounts for this pattern?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Competitive inhibition, because \\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged and \\(K_m\\) increases"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Uncompetitive inhibition, because \\(V_{max}\\) and \\(K_m\\) both decrease"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Noncompetitive inhibition, because \\(V_{max}\\) decreases and \\(K_m\\) is unchanged"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mixed inhibition, because \\(V_{max}\\) decreases and \\(K_m\\) increases"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, the y-intercept equals \(1/V_{max}\) and the x-intercept equals \(-1/K_m\), so a shared y-intercept means \(V_{max}\) is unchanged, while a shift of the x-intercept toward the origin (from \(-0.5\) to \(-0.2\ \text{mM}^{-1}\)) means \(1/K_m\) got smaller and therefore \(K_m\) increased. The text states that for competitive inhibition the plots are ''a series of lines, with the same y-intercept (1/VM), and the x-intercepts (-1/KM) closer and closer to 0 as I increases'' (line 27846), the hallmark of competitive inhibition. (Choice A) is correct: an unchanged \(V_{max}\) with an increased apparent \(K_m\) is exactly the competitive pattern, because the inhibitor competes for the active site and is overcome at saturating \([S]\). (Choice B) is incorrect: uncompetitive inhibition decreases both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) and produces parallel lines (line 28122); a student who defaults to uncompetitive ignores that parallel lines never share a y-intercept. (Choice C) is incorrect: (pure) noncompetitive inhibition lowers \(V_{max}\) and leaves \(K_m\) unchanged, giving lines that intersect on the x-axis (line 28244); this reverses which intercept moved, mistaking the shared y-intercept for a shared x-intercept. (Choice D) is incorrect: mixed inhibition changes both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\), so its lines cannot share a y-intercept; a student who over-reads the plot assumes every parameter shifted rather than recognizing the fixed y-intercept fixes \(V_{max}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the intercept meanings of a double-reciprocal plot to two graph features simultaneously and infer the inhibition mechanism.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e3dca137-4c0e-4ba4-b5cc-8da9d888e7a0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e3dca137-4c0e-4ba4-b5cc-8da9d888e7a0';
